    "text": "To determine how many times Enrique will need to shred 6 units of paper until all of his 2,132 contracts are shredded, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of pages in all the contracts:**\n   Each contract is 132 pages long, and Enrique has 2,132 contracts.\n   \\[\n   2,132 \\text{ contracts} \\times 132 \\text{ pages per contract} = 2,821,44 \\text{ pages}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine how many times Enrique needs to shred 6 pages at a time:**\n   Since his shredder can shred 6 pages at a time, we divide the total number of pages by 6.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2,821,44 \\text{ pages}}{6 \\text{ pages per shred}} = 470,24 \\text{ shreds}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verify the calculation:**\n   To ensure the calculation is correct, we can multiply the number of shreds by 6 to see if it equals the total number of pages.\n   \\[\n   470,24 \\text{ shreds} \\times 6 \\text{ pages per shred} = 2,821,44 \\text{ pages}\n   \\]\n   The calculation is correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{47024}\n\\]",
